# Site-wide trailing-slash enforcement: every page route without a
# trailing slash 301-redirects to its trailing-slash version, matching
# the canonical tag / sitemap.xml convention already used across all 33
# pages (see layout.js's renderPage() and build.js's sitemap generation).
#
# This can't be a single unconditional "/*  /:splat/  301!" rule for two
# reasons:
#   1. Netlify's splat (*) matches the whole remaining path including any
#      trailing slash already present, so a request for an already-correct
#      URL like /monchique/ would get redirected to /monchique// (a
#      broken double slash) instead of being left alone.
#   2. The force flag (!) needed to override Netlify's default
#      extensionless "pretty URL" serving (which would otherwise silently
#      serve /monchique at the bare path with no redirect at all) applies
#      to literally anything matching the pattern — including real static
#      files like /assets/css/main.css, which would then get force-
#      redirected to a nonexistent trailing-slash path and break.
#
# So: explicitly pass through real assets and already-slashed paths first
# (first matching rule wins, so the forced catch-all below never sees
# them), then the one forced rule at the bottom handles every remaining
# page route uniformly — still a single global pattern for the actual
# redirect, not one rule per page.

/                        /                        200
/assets/*                /assets/:splat           200
/favicon.ico             /favicon.ico             200
/apple-touch-icon.png    /apple-touch-icon.png    200
/robots.txt              /robots.txt              200
/sitemap.xml             /sitemap.xml             200
/*/                      /:splat/                 200

/*                       /:splat/                 301!
